WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.559
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B one.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.164
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:15.960
Unit thirty-nine: Review: B1 Grammar. Today you will review the main B1 grammar points.

4
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.807
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:23.034
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:25.001
Present perfect.

7
00:00:26.017 --> 00:00:29.258
Present perfect means have / has + past participle.

8
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.025
I have finished.

9
00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:34.606
Your turn. Say: Present perfect.

10
00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:38.712
Present perfect.

11
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.625
Conditionals.

12
00:00:41.617 --> 00:00:43.674
Conditionals means if sentences.

13
00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:46.202
If I study, I will pass.

14
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:49.376
Your turn. Say: Conditionals.

15
00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:53.455
Conditionals.

16
00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:55.020
Passive.

17
00:00:56.017 --> 00:00:58.469
Passive means be + past participle.

18
00:00:59.083 --> 00:01:00.201
The bridge was built.

19
00:01:00.817 --> 00:01:03.411
Your turn. Say: Passive.

20
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:07.082
Passive.

21
00:01:07.817 --> 00:01:09.259
Reported speech.

22
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.784
Reported speech means telling what others said.

23
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:14.907
She said she was tired.

24
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:18.575
Your turn. Say: Reported speech.

25
00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:22.771
Reported speech.

26
00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:25.005
Relative clauses.

27
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:28.655
Relative clauses means who, which, that, where.

28
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.518
The man who called.

29
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:34.303
Your turn. Say: Relative clauses.

30
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:38.771
Relative clauses.

31
00:01:39.517 --> 00:01:40.378
Modals.

32
00:01:41.383 --> 00:01:44.544
Modals means can, must, might, should.

33
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.325
You must go.

34
00:01:46.917 --> 00:01:49.383
Your turn. Say: Modals.

35
00:01:52.233 --> 00:01:53.246
Modals.

36
00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:55.182
Linking words.

37
00:01:56.183 --> 00:01:58.833
Linking words means although, however, because.

38
00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:00.650
Although it rained.

39
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.611
Your turn. Say: Linking words.

40
00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:08.758
Linking words.

41
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:10.680
Future forms.

42
00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:15.227
Future forms means will, going to, present continuous.

43
00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:17.096
I'm going to study.

44
00:02:17.683 --> 00:02:20.591
Your turn. Say: Future forms.

45
00:02:23.433 --> 00:02:24.539
Future forms.

46
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:28.752
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:29.617 --> 00:02:32.742
Visal, which B1 grammar point helps you most in writing?

48
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:45.465
Linking words and relative clauses help me write longer, clearer sentences. B1 grammar gives you tools to talk about time, possibility, rules and opinion.

49
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.375
Can you list the key areas?

50
00:02:49.483 --> 00:03:01.760
Perfect tenses, three conditionals, the passive, reported speech, relative clauses, modals, linking words and future forms. Together they make your English more accurate.

51
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.269
How do we review well?

52
00:03:05.383 --> 00:03:18.234
Write your own examples and check them. Use the grammar in speaking too. Mistakes are useful, because each one shows what to practise next. Next you will meet B2 grammar.

53
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.121
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:21.983 --> 00:03:24.726
Grammar focus: B1 grammar review.

55
00:03:25.026 --> 00:03:26.329
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:27.017 --> 00:03:30.340
Present perfect have + past participle I have finished.

57
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.294
Passive be + past participle it was built.

58
00:03:35.183 --> 00:03:38.553
Conditional if + past, would If I won, I would travel.

59
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:47.876
Review the perfect tenses, conditionals, passive, reported speech, relative clauses, modals, linking words and future forms. Write your own examples.

60
00:03:48.617 --> 00:03:52.384
Be careful! Don't say: I have seen him yesterday.

61
00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:56.333
Say: I saw him yesterday.

62
00:03:56.633 --> 00:03:58.052
Use the past simple.

63
00:03:58.867 --> 00:04:01.322
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:03.460
B1 in Review.

65
00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.721
B1 grammar helps you talk about time, rules and opinions.

66
00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:11.267
The present perfect links the past to the present.

67
00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:16.170
Conditionals, passives and reported speech add variety to your writing.

68
00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:20.676
Linking words and relative clauses make longer sentences clear.

69
00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:24.270
Every mistake shows what to practise next.

70
00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:29.484
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:32.535
Which tense links past and present?

72
00:04:32.835 --> 00:04:37.592
A, present perfect. B, past simple. C, future perfect.

73
00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:43.539
The answer is A: present perfect.

74
00:04:44.683 --> 00:04:46.648
If I blank rich, I would travel.

75
00:04:46.948 --> 00:04:50.056
A, am. B, were. C, will be.

76
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.333
The answer is B: were.

77
00:04:56.483 --> 00:04:58.390
'She said she was tired' is blank.

78
00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.040
A, reported speech. B, a question tag. C, a passive.

79
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.203
The answer is A: reported speech.

80
00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.512
Which is a linking word of contrast?

81
00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:16.036
A, although. B, because. C, first.

82
00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.240
The answer is A: although.

83
00:05:22.383 --> 00:05:25.683
According to the reading, what shows what to practise next?

84
00:05:25.983 --> 00:05:30.089
A, every mistake. B, a test. C, a teacher.

85
00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:36.341
The answer is A: every mistake.

86
00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:40.894
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:44.655
Which B1 grammar point helps you most in writing?

88
00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:54.869
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:55.169 --> 00:06:08.189
Relative clauses help me most in writing, because they let me add information without starting a new sentence. I also use linking words like although and however to compare ideas.

90
00:06:08.489 --> 00:06:10.245
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:15.567
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and b1 grammar review.

92
00:06:15.867 --> 00:06:20.168
Today's value is lifelong learning. Keep learning for as long as you live.

93
00:06:20.468 --> 00:06:27.303
Congratulations! You have finished Online Grammar, level B one. See you in Online Grammar, level B two!

94
00:06:27.603 --> 00:06:30.603
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

